  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, cool, and breezy conditions continue \nthrough Thursday. \n\nNot anticipating much change to the forecast through Thursday. \nA sprawling high pressure to our north will maintain cooler \nconditions with gusty east to northeast winds. There should be a\nbrief period this evening where gusts subside before another \nsurge of colder air starts the gustiness again. The strongest \ngusts should be from late morning into the afternoon Wednesday. \nSlightly less breezy conditions for Thursday.\n\nAcross the southern coastal waters, Gale Warnings remain in \neffect for Wednesday and Thursday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Monitoring the potential for a strong coastal \nlow to arrive late week, bringing chances for strong winds, \nrainfall, and coastal flood/erosion concerns through the \nweekend.\n\nWhile there is still a large amount of uncertainty in the \nguidance, the overarching trend is indicating the low slowly \nmeanders north and retrograding west into the waters south of \nBlock Island and Nantucket; however, how close the low tracks to\nthe southern coast is still a large question. It is important \nto note that the exact track and strength of the low will \nstrongly influence the timing and magnitude of gusts, QPF, and \nhow far inland the rain extends. The latest ECMWF is indicating \nthe low passes closer to Long Island whereas the latest GFS is \nshowing the low passing closer to the outer Cape and Nantucket. \nDepending on the track the low takes, the onset of the strongest\nwinds could be as early as Friday evening or as late as \nSaturday afternoon. Regardless, confidence is slowly increasing \nin at least Gale Force winds (34kts/40mph), with the possibility\nof Storm Force winds (48kts/55mph) Friday night/Saturday, \nacross the southern waters leading to an upgrade of the Gale \nWatch to a Gale Warning for the southern and southeastern \nwaters.\n\nThis prolonged period of gusty northeasterly winds will build \nrough seas and raise the threat for coastal flooding and \nerosion, especially for east facing beaches and harbors. Latest \ncoastal flooding guidance from the Stevens Institute shows \ngreatest risk for at least minor coastal flooding for Nantucket \nHarbor around the high tidal cycles Friday, Friday night, and \nSaturday. The coastal flooding risk is slowly increasing for \nBoston Harbor as well, although to a slightly lesser extent \ncompared to Nantucket.",
